## Why This Is Asked

Assess practical power-aware location handling in RN apps, using AppState and battery APIs, plus a simple UI.

## Key Concepts

- AppState
- Battery level access
- Polling strategies
- Permissions (LOCATION)

## Code Example

```javascript
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { AppState } from 'react-native';
import { getBatteryLevel } from 'react-native-device-info';

function useLocationWithBatteryGuard() {
  const [coords, setCoords] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    let interval = null;
    const determineInterval = async () => {
      const lvl = await getBatteryLevel();
      if (lvl > 0.8) return 1000;
      if (lvl > 0.2) return 5000;
      return 15000;
    };
    const start = async () => {
      const ms = await determineInterval();
      interval = setInterval(() => {
        // request location here
      }, ms);
    };
    start();
    const subscription = AppState.addEventListener('change', async (state) => {
      if (state === 'background') clearInterval(interval);
      else {
        const ms = await determineInterval();
        interval = setInterval(() => {
          // request location here
        }, ms);
      }
    });
    return () => {
      clearInterval(interval);
      subscription.remove();
    };
  }, []);
  return coords;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this on real devices?
- How would you adjust for location permission revocation at runtime?
- How would you handle rapid battery level changes during a session?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to build a basic, performative list in RN with local data, search, skeleton loading, and rendering optimizations. Also checks understanding of stable item keys and accessibility.

## Key Concepts
- FlatList virtualization
- useMemo and React.memo
- keyExtractor and getItemLayout
- basic accessibility

## Code Example
```javascript
import React, { useMemo, useState, useCallback } from 'react';
import { FlatList, TextInput, View, Text } from 'react-native';

const Item = React.memo(({ name }) => <Text accessibilityLabel={`item-${name}`}>{name}</Text>);

export default function ItemList({ data }) {
  const [q, setQ] = useState('');
  const filtered = useMemo(() => data.filter(i => i.name.toLowerCase().includes(q.toLowerCase())), [data, q]);
  const renderItem = useCallback(({ item }) => <Item name={item.name} />, []);
  return (
    <View>
      <TextInput value={q} onChangeText={setQ} placeholder="Search" accessibilityLabel="search" />
      <FlatList
        data={filtered}
        keyExtractor={item => item.id}
        renderItem={renderItem}
        getItemLayout={(_, index) => ({ length: 48, offset: 48 * index, index })}
        initialNumToRender={20}
      />
    </View>
  );
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you debounce the search input and what would you test for performance?
- How would you enhance accessibility and localization for item text?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates cross-platform native module design, real-time streaming data handling, memory management, and privacy considerations in mobile development.

### Key Concepts
- TurboModule/JSI bridge for high-performance native interop
- Real-time frame streaming with backpressure handling
- Buffer reuse and memory management
- Privacy: ephemeral buffers only, no raw frame storage

### Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal usage sketch
import OCR from 'react-native-ocr';

OCR.start({ 
  cropRegion: { x: 0.1, y: 0.1, w: 0.8, h: 0.8 }, 
  language: 'en' 
});

OCR.onFrame((payload) => {
  const { blocks, boundingBoxes, timestamp } = payload;
  blocks.forEach(block => {
    console.log('Text:', block.text);
    console.log('Bounding Box:', block.boundingBox);
  });
});

// Lifecycle management
OCR.pause();
OCR.resume();
OCR.stop();
```

### Implementation Considerations

**Memory Management:**
- Reuse image buffers between frames to avoid allocation overhead
- Use ring buffers for event emission to prevent memory growth
- Release native resources immediately on stop()

**Backpressure:**
- Drop frames when processing cannot keep pace
- Use a bounded queue with configurable maximum size
- Implement a strategy to skip intermediate frames rather than queuing

**Privacy:**
- Process frames in memory only; never persist raw camera data
- Discard frames immediately after OCR processing completes
- Avoid logging or exporting raw image content

**Lifecycle:**
- Automatically pause on app backgrounding
- Resume processing on foreground
- Clean up all native resources and event listeners on stop()

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical push handling and navigation restoration in a RN app with real user flows and edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Push notification payload handling (FCM/APNs) and difference between data and notification payloads
- Navigation state restoration and a central NavigationService
- Debouncing, idempotent navigation, and duplicate avoidance
- AsyncStorage for lastRoute and a simple queue
- Testing strategies for foreground/background/killed states

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal hook sketch
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import AsyncStorage from '@react-native-async-storage/async-storage';
import { getNavigationRef } from './NavigationService';

function mapPayloadToRoute(p) {
  // implement payload-to-route mapping
  const route = p?.targetRoute;
  return route || null;
}

export function useNotifyNavigate() {
  const nav = getNavigationRef();
  const timer = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const onNotify = (payload) => {
      const route = mapPayloadToRoute(payload);
      if (!route) return;
      clearTimeout(timer.current);
      timer.current = setTimeout(async () => {
        const last = await AsyncStorage.getItem('lastRoute');
        if (last === route) return;
        await AsyncStorage.setItem('lastRoute', route);
        nav?.navigate(route);
      }, 150);
    };
    NotificationCenter.addListener('notify', onNotify);
    return () => NotificationCenter.removeListener('notify', onNotify);
  }, [nav]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions between fast successive notifications?
- How would you adapt for multi-window or deep linking across platforms?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical offline-first data handling in React Native. It probes AsyncStorage usage, hook design, and clear cache invalidation logic that affects UX.

## Key Concepts
- AsyncStorage caching with TTL
- Custom React hook design: useCachedQuery
- Offline-first UX and error handling
- FlatList integration for data rendering

## Code Example
```javascript
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import AsyncStorage from '@react-native-async-storage/async-storage';

export function useCachedQuery(key, fetchFn, ttl) {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  const load = async () => {
    setLoading(true);
    try {
      const cached = await AsyncStorage.getItem(key);
      if (cached) {
        const { value, ts } = JSON.parse(cached);
        if (Date.now() - ts < ttl) {
          setData(value);
          setLoading(false);
          return;
        }
      }
      const fresh = await fetchFn();
      await AsyncStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify({ value: fresh, ts: Date.now() }));
      setData(fresh);
    } catch (e) {
      setError(e);
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  useEffect(() => { load(); }, [key]);
  return { data, loading, error, refetch: load };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust TTL to balance freshness and offline availability?
- How would you test offline-first behavior in a RN app?
